<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chalcuchímac</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1533">1533</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chalcuchímac was one of the leading generals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a> in the civil war against his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huascar">Huáscar</a>. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quizquiz">Quizquiz</a> he defeated Huáscar's army near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> in 1532 and captured him. After Atahualpa's capture he submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a>, but the Spaniards accused him of plotting and burned him alive in 1533.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chalcuchimac.jpg" alt="Chalcuchímac" width="320" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guaman Poma de Ayala · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Challco_Chima.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Chalcuchímac, originally written Challcochima or Challcuchima, was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, at the northern end of the empire, in the latter part of the 15th century. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quizquiz">Quizquiz</a> and Rumiñawi he was one of the leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> generals of the north. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac">Huayna Capac</a> died in 1527 and his intended heir Ninan Cuyochi died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a>, the empire divided, and as a man of the north Chalcuchímac swore allegiance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the civil war that followed from 1529, he fought alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a> against his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huascar">Huáscar</a>, sharing in the victory at the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chimborazo">Chimborazo</a>, after which Huáscar's general and brother Atoc was captured, mutilated and killed. In April 1532 he and his companion defeated and captured Huáscar at the battle of Quipaipan.</p><p>After the Spaniards seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a> at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cajamarca">Cajamarca</a>, Chalcuchímac was camped with an army of 35,000 in the Jauja Valley. Hernando Pizarro persuaded him that Atahualpa had summoned him to Cajamarca, and there the Spaniards arrested him, fearing that he might renew hostilities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Once <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a> had been executed on 29 July 1533, Pizarro marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a> with five hundred Spaniards, taking Chalcuchímac with him. The native forces attacked the column several times with such spirit and discipline that the Spaniards suspected him of directing them in secret, and a rumour spread that he had told <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quizquiz">Quizquiz</a> the Spanish force was divided. The suspicions were never proved, but they decided his fate: Pizarro sentenced him to be burned alive. Offered a less painful death if he became a Christian, he refused baptism and died as sentenced, protesting to the last against the injustice of his condemnation. The Spaniards later routed Quizquiz's forces and took Cuzco late in 1533.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chalcuchima” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Atahualpa’s generals Quizquiz and <b>Chalcuchímac</b> defeated Huáscar’s forces in 1532 near Cusco and captured him, and Atahualpa’s troops killed many of Huáscar’s relatives and supporters.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ecuador">Ecuador</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-0.22,-78.51&amp;year=1523" aria-label="Quito on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2369 2172 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Chalcuchímac"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2819 2442h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2819 2442h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-0.22,-78.51&amp;year=1523">What was here in 1523? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
